            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus primarily views the great fish (especially in Jonah) as a typological symbol of Christ's tomb, the realm of death (Sheol), or a divine instrument of deliverance. A secondary or minority view, usually associated with the broader concept of 'sea-monsters,' identifies such creatures as symbols of primordial chaos or hostile empires.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines the great fish or sea-monster as a hostile earthly ruler who acts as the head of an empire (such as Pharaoh, Nebuchadnezzar, or Caesar), swallowing victims but ultimately being forced to disgorge them.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A typological figure for the grave/tomb of Christ, or an instrument of divine rescue.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A great ruler or empire that swallows and is forced to release its victims.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book rejects the primary consensus view of the fish as a symbol of the tomb or divine deliverance, instead universally applying a minority prophetic motif where sea-monsters represent oppressive political empires and their rulers.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The primary consensus identifies the great fish as the grave, death, or a means of salvation (Christ's tomb); the book identifies it as a hostile human ruler or political state (e.g., Caesar or Pharaoh).\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By explicitly acknowledging the 'commonly taught' view of the fish as a type of the tomb/rescue instrument while offering an entirely different definition, the book replaces the primary consensus referent with a political/imperial one. Although this political reading exists as a minority view in the consensus for 'sea-monsters,' elevating it as the primary definition for the 'Great Fish' contradicts the dominant Christological consensus.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In Matthew 12:40, Jesus explicitly compares Jonah's time in the fish to his own time 'in the heart of the earth' (the tomb), which strongly anchors the grave/tomb typology. Furthermore, Jonah's fish is portrayed as an obedient instrument of God's rescue rather than a hostile, rebellious oppressor.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
